# ── Multi-host deployment ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# TWO hostnames front ONE janux. Both must be reachable from the client over
# https (caddy's local CA; up.py maps both into /etc/hosts and trusts that CA
# on macOS). Both names are added to `JANUX_HOSTS` (compose.yml) so janux
# resolves the tenant for each.
#
#   https://auth.example.com — janux whole surface (login UI, OIDC, admin,
#                              SCIM, health). Public.
#   https://app.example.com  — a static file, gated by forward-auth.
#
# TLS: `tls internal` below (per site) makes caddy sign with its local CA.
# NOTE: this caddy build does NOT honor the CADDY_TLS env var, and its
# caddyfile adapter rejects a `global { tls internal }` block; the working
# form is `tls internal` inside each site block. That is what up.py's
# `trust-ca` then extracts from data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/.
#
# ── Sub-domain SSO via a Domain-scoped cookie ──────────────────────────
# The session cookie is Host-only BY DEFAULT (src/verify.rs::set_session_cookie):
# a browser sends it back only to the host that SET it. In this layout the
# login is done on `auth.example.com`, so a host-only cookie would NOT be sent
# to `app.example.com` and the gate would bounce on every /app.
#
# That is why this compose sets BOTH domains' `cookie_scope = "example.com"`
# (examples/split-hosts/seed.example.toml). janux then writes
# `Set-Cookie: ...; Domain=example.com`, so the browser treats
# auth.example.com and app.example.com as one site and shares ONE login
# across both hosts. The scope is validated at seed time by an RFC 6265
# 5.1.3 label-aligned match (NOT substring) so it can never leak to a
# sibling registrable domain. See `session.cookie_scope` below in the seed.
#
# ── The redirect on unauth ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# `base.toml` sets `forward_auth_redirect = true`, so an UNAUTHENTICATED
# /app probe gets janux's 303. janux resolves the 303 target from the
# request's tenant-scoped `session.redirect_url` and appends /login:
# app.example.com points back at auth.example.com (see the seed), so a
# bounced /app lands on the AUTH host's login UI, then the Domain-scoped
# cookie makes the post-login /app succeed. Caddy propagates the 303 to the
# browser. See ../README.md.
# A `global { tls internal }` block is rejected by this caddy build's caddyfile
# adapter, so TLS is configured per site instead: see the `tls internal` line
# in the app + auth blocks below.

# ── The app host: static file gated by forward-auth ─────────────────────
app.example.com {
	# Use caddy's local CA (self-signed) so https://app.example.com works
	# without a real, public cert.
	tls internal

	# The demo "service": a static file gated by forward-auth. A successful (2xx)
	# probe falls through to nginx; an unauthenticated probe yields janux's 303
	# -> /login, propagated to the browser below.
	@appName path /app
	handle @appName {
		forward_auth auth:8080 {
			uri /api/v1/auth/verify
			# janux runs its DEFAULT-DENY engine on the PROXIED request: it reads
			# X-Forwarded-{Host,Uri,Method} and evaluates the tenant's policy for
			# resource "/app" (seeded in seed.toml). 200 -> allow; 401 -> unauth;
			# 403 -> the who/is/for triple lacks permission.
		}
		reverse_proxy app:80
	}
}

# ── The auth host: janux's full surface, public ─────────────────────────
auth.example.com {
	# Same local CA so the browser trusts auth.example.com too.
	tls internal

	handle {
		reverse_proxy auth:8080
	}
}
